workedExamples.solution_text:
$y'=3(x+2)^2$.

Set $y'=0$:
$\begin{aligned}3(x+2)^2&=0\\x+2&=0\\x&=-2\end{aligned}$

$y=(-2+2)^3-1=-1$.

The stationary point is $(-2,-1)$. The tangent there is horizontal.

[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{mathblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[<->,black] (-3.7,0)--(0.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-3.1)--(0,1.1) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[mathblue,thick,<->,domain=-3.25:-0.75,samples=81] plot (\x,{(\x+2)*(\x+2)*(\x+2)-1});
\draw[black,dashed] (-2,0)--(-2,-1);
\draw[black] (-2.65,-1)--(-1.35,-1);
\fill[black] (-2,-1) circle (1.2pt);
\path (-2,-1) node[below right] {$(-2,-1)$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
